cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Covered but excluded primes: which primes divide an element of { n : rad(n) | f(n) }"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Ellena Godoy"
    given-names: "Jorge"
    email: jorgell23@gmail.com
abstract: >-
  For a multiplicative f with f(q^e) = F(q^e) and F(0) = +-1, a prime has an
  incoming arrow in the covering digraph of f exactly when F has a root modulo
  that prime, and the density of the primes without one is the proportion of
  fixed-point-free elements of the Galois group of F (Frobenius, 1896, applied
  to this object). Having an incoming arrow is necessary but NOT sufficient for
  a prime to divide an element of S(f) = { n : rad(n) | f(n) }: for
  f(q^e) = q^(2e) - q^e + 1 the prime 3 is covered and divides nothing. Every
  such failure occurs at a prime ramified in the splitting field of F, hence at
  finitely many primes per F. Primes on a directed cycle do divide an element,
  with a finite certificate.
keywords:
  - number theory
  - arithmetic functions
  - covering digraph
  - Frobenius density theorem
  - prime-perfect numbers
  - radical
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/jorgell23-sys/covered-but-excluded-primes"
url: "https://jorgell23-sys.github.io/covered-but-excluded-primes/"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-09-06
